# Line endings are a correctness matter here, not a preference.
#
# A shell script with CRLF endings fails on Linux with a confusing interpreter error, and a batch
# file with LF endings can mis-parse the parenthesised blocks cmd.exe uses for if/else. Both are
# checked in from a Windows machine, so neither can be left to whatever the working copy happens to
# have.
# Git hooks are shell scripts with no extension, so the *.sh rule below does not reach them. A
# hook checked out with CRLF fails with a "bad interpreter" error that names the shell rather than
# the line endings, which is a long way to walk for a wrong newline.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
